 ## Overview
-GART is a method that extends the human to more articulated models (e.g., animals) based on the 3DGS representation [S1][S2, unofficial]. It builds on [Human and Articulated Avatars](8209-human-and-articulated-avatars.md) by generalizing the canonical-space deformation framework from bipedal humans to other articulated species.
+GART is a 3D Gaussian Splatting-based approach designed to animate articulated non-human models, such as animals, by extending the canonical space deformation principles originally developed for human avatars [S1][S2, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods like [GaussianHead](8051-gaussianhead.md) or [D3GA](8094-d3ga.md) which focus on specific human facial or body parts, GART generalizes the template-guided parameterization to broader articulated skeletons [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of realistic, real-time rendering of complex, non-human articulated figures by mapping canonical 3D Gaussians to target poses via a differentiable deformation function [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** Multi-view images of an articulated non-human subject (e.g., animal); camera poses; initial canonical 3D Gaussian parameters (position, covariance, opacity, color, SH coefficients) in a reference pose.
-*   **Outputs:** Rendered images; optimized Gaussian attributes; deformation parameters mapping canonical Gaussians to target poses.
